**Read:** [Electron-excited X-ray microanalysis by EDS at 50 — Newbury and Ritchie](../sources/NIST-Quantitative-EDS.lingua#r_7XJDCAH01NX65F6Q5YY3CBFTCF), the complete relevant mapping and quantitative-analysis treatment; [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the full relevant economic-mineral and texture sections; [Handbook of Mineralogy — Anthony, Bideaux, Bladh, and Nichols](../sources/Handbook-of-Mineralogy.lingua#r_10TEF3RSN7XFMKTGYGY4WR3P85), complete selected mineral entries and source references.

Process mineralogy connects mineral identity/composition, grain size and association, intergrowths, alteration, and particle structure with recovery and product quality. A valuable element may be hosted in several phases or substitutions requiring different pathways. Grade and mineralogical accessibility answer different questions.

Liberation describes separation of a target phase within particles under an identified definition and particle-size population. A two-dimensional section can intersect a three-dimensional intergrowth in a misleading way. Modal mineral proportions, surface exposure, mass liberation, and recovery under a process are distinct quantities.

Image classification, density/volume conversion, sampling, particle selection, analytical interaction volume, and unresolved fine material affect estimates. A large number of classified pixels does not eliminate a biased particle population or systematic mineral misclassification. Independent bulk/phase balances can test the account.

**Work:** reconstruct an authorized published process-mineralogy case from sample selection through imaging, phase assignment, stereological assumptions, and interpretation. Compare a discrete-grain and a fine-intergrowth material with similar bulk grade. Explain how a changed comminution/selection scheme could alter exposure or association, while identifying the additional experiments needed to assess recovery and energy.

Read the complete relevant characterization and mineral-group treatments and original case paper. The later mineral-processing trail will develop comminution and separation design, keeping this shared material evidence instead of assuming every ore or industrial mineral behaves alike.

